import React from 'react';
import PageTemplate from '../components/PageTemplate';

import costVsWait from '../images/fleet-optimization/01_cost_vs_wait.png';
import optionComparison from '../images/fleet-optimization/02_option_comparison.png';
import dailyTimeline from '../images/fleet-optimization/03_daily_timeline.png';
import hourlyProfile from '../images/fleet-optimization/05_hourly_profile.png';
import waitDistribution from '../images/fleet-optimization/06_wait_distribution.png';
import monteCarloPrecision from '../images/fleet-optimization/07_monte_carlo_precision.png';
import demandSensitivity from '../images/fleet-optimization/08_demand_sensitivity.png';

const tableStyle = { margin: '20px 0', borderCollapse: 'collapse', width: '100%' };
const headRowStyle = { borderBottom: '2px solid #333' };
const rowStyle = { borderBottom: '1px solid #ddd' };
const thStyle = { padding: '10px', textAlign: 'left' };
const thRight = { padding: '10px', textAlign: 'right' };
const tdStyle = { padding: '10px' };
const tdRight = { padding: '10px', textAlign: 'right' };

export default function FleetOptimizationPage() {
  return (
    <PageTemplate
      title="Fleet Sizing with Monte Carlo Simulation: The Cheapest Fleet That Holds Up Day After Day"
      metadata={[
        { label: 'Role', value: 'Simulation and optimization' },
        { label: 'Stack', value: 'Python, NumPy, discrete-event simulation, Monte Carlo' },
        { label: 'Context', value: 'Real shuttle operation of Transportadora Viajes Recreativos, Mexico City' },
        { label: 'Outcome', value: '27 fleets x 10,000 simulated days; recommended fleet meets the 10-minute target on 99.4% of days for $1,091/day' }
      ]}
    >
      <p>
        Transportadora Viajes Recreativos, a passenger transport company in Mexico City, runs a shuttle from 6:00 to 22:00 with a mix of buses and vans. Each vehicle type has its own capacity, shift and hourly cost, and only a few of each are available. The question: which fleet keeps passengers waiting under 10 minutes, at the lowest daily cost?
      </p>
      <p>
        Demand is random and vehicle trips vary, so a single deterministic calculation can't answer that. I built a discrete-event simulation on the company's recorded trip data and ran every feasible fleet through the same 10,000 simulated days: <strong>270,000 simulated days</strong> in total.
      </p>
      <p>
        The answer turned out to depend on what "under 10 minutes" means. The cheapest fleet whose long-run average is under 10 minutes misses the target on more than a fifth of individual days. So I required the target to hold on 95% of days, which points to a different fleet for about $116 more per day.
      </p>

      <h2>The Problem</h2>
      <table style={tableStyle}>
        <thead>
          <tr style={headRowStyle}>
            <th style={thStyle}>Vehicle</th>
            <th style={thRight}>Seats</th>
            <th style={thStyle}>Shift</th>
            <th style={thRight}>Cost per hour</th>
            <th style={thRight}>Available</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr style={rowStyle}><td style={tdStyle}>Bus A</td><td style={tdRight}>37</td><td style={tdStyle}>6:00 to 17:00</td><td style={tdRight}>$42.60</td><td style={tdRight}>1</td></tr>
          <tr style={rowStyle}><td style={tdStyle}>Bus B</td><td style={tdRight}>31</td><td style={tdStyle}>8:00 to 14:00</td><td style={tdRight}>$42.60</td><td style={tdRight}>2</td></tr>
          <tr style={rowStyle}><td style={tdStyle}>Van A</td><td style={tdRight}>19</td><td style={tdStyle}>6:00 to 20:00</td><td style={tdRight}>$24.80</td><td style={tdRight}>1</td></tr>
          <tr style={rowStyle}><td style={tdStyle}>Van B</td><td style={tdRight}>13</td><td style={tdStyle}>7:00 to 22:00</td><td style={tdRight}>$24.80</td><td style={tdRight}>3</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        A fleet must have at least one vehicle on duty at every minute from 6:00 to 22:00 and can't use more vehicles than exist. 27 of the 47 possible combinations meet both conditions. Each vehicle is paid for its whole shift, so a fleet's daily cost is fixed. Its waiting time is random.
      </p>

      <h2>The Model</h2>
      <h3>Passengers</h3>
      <p>
        Passengers arrive as a non-homogeneous Poisson process: random arrivals at an average rate that changes through the day. On average, one passenger arrives every 3.0 minutes from 6 to 8, every 2.0 from 8 to 10, every 3.0 from 10 to 13, every 1.6 during the afternoon peak (13 to 17) and every 2.6 in the evening. That is about 425 passengers a day. Because the rate is constant within each window, the process can be sampled exactly: draw a Poisson count for the window, then place that many arrivals uniformly inside it.
      </p>

      <h3>Vehicles</h3>
      <p>
        Every vehicle makes its first trip at the start of its shift. The time until its next departure is resampled from real gaps between departures, which the company recorded in four windows (6 to 9, 9 to 14, 14 to 18 and 18 to 22). So the simulated schedules vary the same way the real ones do.
      </p>

      <h3>Queueing</h3>
      <p>
        All trips of a fleet are merged in time order. Passengers queue first come, first served, and each departing vehicle boards up to its capacity. Anyone still waiting at 22:00 is counted as unserved. The time-average queue length comes from Little's law: the total minutes passengers spent waiting, divided by the length of the day.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each simulated day yields the average wait, the 95th percentile and maximum wait per passenger, the average and maximum queue, trips, occupancy and unserved passengers.
      </p>

      <h2>Monte Carlo with Common Random Numbers</h2>
      <p>
        The decision depends on <em>differences</em> between fleets, and those differences are small next to the day-to-day noise. If each fleet saw its own random days, a fleet could look better just because it got quieter days.
      </p>
      <p>
        So every fleet is simulated on the same days. Day <em>k</em> has the same passenger arrivals in every fleet, and each individual vehicle has its own random stream: a given van drives the same schedule on day <em>k</em> in every fleet that includes it. The noise that both fleets share cancels when they are compared.
      </p>
      <p>
        To measure the gain, I also simulated every fleet on 2,000 days with independent random inputs. With common random numbers, the variance of the difference between each fleet and the recommended one is a median of <strong>2.0x smaller</strong>, and up to 9.6x smaller for fleets that share most of their vehicles. Each paired comparison over 10,000 days is as precise as about 20,000 independent days.
      </p>
      <img src={monteCarloPrecision} alt="Convergence of the mean wait and variance reduction from common random numbers" className="page-image" />
      <p className="image-caption">Left: the recommended fleet's mean wait settles as days accumulate. The 95% confidence interval after 10,000 days is ±0.01 minutes. Right: variance reduction from common random numbers, for each fleet compared with the recommended one.</p>
      <p>
        The simulator handles a day in about 100 microseconds, so the full run of 270,000 days takes under 30 seconds and the whole notebook about a minute and a half.
      </p>

      <h2>Results</h2>
      <img src={costVsWait} alt="Daily cost vs. average wait for 27 fleets" className="page-image" />
      <p className="image-caption">Each point is a fleet: its mean wait over 10,000 days, with a bar from the 5th to the 95th percentile of the day's average wait. Color shows the share of days under 10 minutes.</p>
      <p>
        24 of the 27 fleets have a mean wait under 10 minutes, but only 21 stay under 10 minutes on at least 95% of days. The difference matters most at the cheap end, where the decision is made:
      </p>
      <img src={waitDistribution} alt="Distribution of the daily average wait for the 10 cheapest fleets" className="page-image" />
      <p className="image-caption">Distribution of the day's average wait over 10,000 days for the 10 cheapest fleets, with the share of days on target above each one.</p>

      <table style={tableStyle}>
        <thead>
          <tr style={headRowStyle}>
            <th style={thStyle}>Option</th>
            <th style={thStyle}>Fleet</th>
            <th style={thRight}>Daily cost</th>
            <th style={thRight}>Average wait</th>
            <th style={thRight}>Days under 10 min</th>
            <th style={thRight}>95th pct. wait</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr style={rowStyle}><td style={tdStyle}>Cheapest on average</td><td style={tdStyle}>1 Bus B, 1 Van A, 1 Van B</td><td style={tdRight}>$974.80</td><td style={tdRight}>9.43 min</td><td style={tdRight}>78.4%</td><td style={tdRight}>23.0 min</td></tr>
          <tr style={rowStyle}><td style={tdStyle}><strong>Recommended</strong></td><td style={tdStyle}><strong>1 Van A, 2 Van B</strong></td><td style={tdRight}><strong>$1,091.20</strong></td><td style={tdRight}><strong>8.02 min</strong></td><td style={tdRight}><strong>99.4%</strong></td><td style={tdRight}><strong>20.9 min</strong></td></tr>
          <tr style={rowStyle}><td style={tdStyle}>Fastest</td><td style={tdStyle}>1 Bus A, 2 Bus B, 1 Van A, 3 Van B</td><td style={tdRight}>$2,443.00</td><td style={tdRight}>5.08 min</td><td style={tdRight}>100%</td><td style={tdRight}>14.4 min</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div className="highlight-box">
        <p>
          <strong>Recommendation: 1 Van A and 2 Van B, at $1,091.20 a day.</strong> It costs about $42,500 a year more than the cheapest fleet that meets the target on average, and raises the share of days on target from 78% to 99%. It costs about $493,000 a year less than the fastest fleet, which saves 2.9 minutes of average wait.
        </p>
      </div>

      <img src={optionComparison} alt="Comparison of the three options: cost, waits, composition and days on target" className="page-image" />
      <p className="image-caption">The three options side by side: daily cost, average and 95th percentile wait, fleet composition, and share of days on target.</p>

      <h3>Frequency, not capacity</h3>
      <p>
        The recommended fleet has no buses. Averaged over 500 days, its vehicles fill only about 29% of their seats per trip. With seats to spare, a passenger's wait depends on how soon the next vehicle arrives, not on how big it is. Three vans cost the same as a bus-heavy fleet like 1 Bus A, 1 Bus B and 1 Van B ($1,096 a day), but they come by more often: 8.0 minutes of average wait against 10.6.
      </p>
      <img src={hourlyProfile} alt="Queue and occupancy by hour for the recommended fleet" className="page-image" />
      <p className="image-caption">Average queue and occupancy by hour for the recommended fleet over 500 days. The afternoon peak (13:00 to 17:00) has the longest queues and the fullest vehicles.</p>
      <img src={dailyTimeline} alt="One simulated day with the recommended fleet" className="page-image" />
      <p className="image-caption">One simulated day traced trip by trip: passengers boarded per trip (top) and the queue each time a vehicle departs (bottom).</p>

      <h2>How Much Growth the Fleet Can Absorb</h2>
      <p>
        A fleet that barely meets today's demand is a bad buy if demand grows. I scaled every arrival rate up to 3x and reran the study (2,000 days per fleet and level, again with common random numbers):
      </p>
      <table style={tableStyle}>
        <thead>
          <tr style={headRowStyle}>
            <th style={thStyle}>Demand</th>
            <th style={thStyle}>Cheapest fleet on target</th>
            <th style={thRight}>Daily cost</th>
            <th style={thRight}>Recommended fleet: days on target</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr style={rowStyle}><td style={tdStyle}>1x</td><td style={tdStyle}>1 Van A, 2 Van B</td><td style={tdRight}>$1,091.20</td><td style={tdRight}>99.6%</td></tr>
          <tr style={rowStyle}><td style={tdStyle}>1.25x</td><td style={tdStyle}>1 Van A, 2 Van B</td><td style={tdRight}>$1,091.20</td><td style={tdRight}>99.4%</td></tr>
          <tr style={rowStyle}><td style={tdStyle}>1.5x</td><td style={tdStyle}>1 Van A, 2 Van B</td><td style={tdRight}>$1,091.20</td><td style={tdRight}>98.2%</td></tr>
          <tr style={rowStyle}><td style={tdStyle}>2x</td><td style={tdStyle}>1 Bus B, 1 Van A, 2 Van B</td><td style={tdRight}>$1,346.80</td><td style={tdRight}>67.7%</td></tr>
          <tr style={rowStyle}><td style={tdStyle}>2.5x</td><td style={tdStyle}>1 Van A, 3 Van B</td><td style={tdRight}>$1,463.20</td><td style={tdRight}>1.3%</td></tr>
          <tr style={rowStyle}><td style={tdStyle}>3x</td><td style={tdStyle}>1 Bus A, 1 Van A, 2 Van B</td><td style={tdRight}>$1,559.80</td><td style={tdRight}>0%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The recommended fleet absorbs 50% more demand with almost no change in wait (8.0 to 8.4 minutes), because it has seats to spare. Around twice today's demand, seats start to run out, and the cheapest fleet on target adds a bus.
      </p>
      <img src={demandSensitivity} alt="Share of days on target for every fleet at each demand level" className="page-image" />
      <p className="image-caption">Share of days on target for every fleet (sorted by cost) at each demand level. The black box marks the cheapest fleet that meets the target on 95% of days.</p>

      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Arrival rates are fixed per time window. Day-of-week and seasonal effects are covered only through the demand sensitivity study.</li>
        <li>Departure gaps are resampled from historical records, which assumes future schedules behave like the recorded ones and that gaps don't depend on how full a vehicle is. The 18:00 to 22:00 gaps were estimated from the afternoon records with the company's managers, because that window was not recorded.</li>
        <li>Passengers arriving after the last departure of the day (about 2 a day) are counted as unserved.</li>
        <li>Costs are hourly vehicle costs only, with no fixed or maintenance costs.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What I Took Away</h2>
      <p>
        The most useful change was in the question, not the code. "Average wait under 10 minutes" sounds precise, but a fleet can meet it in the long run and still fail on one day in five. Writing the target as a share of days made the recommendation robust, and it took one extra column in the results.
      </p>
      <p>
        Common random numbers were the other lesson. When the decision rests on comparing alternatives, simulating them on the same random days is close to free and makes every comparison sharper. Here it was worth a median of twice the sample size.
      </p>
    </PageTemplate>
  );
}
